course: dictionary
lesson: 8477
title: Words that travel across the world
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words travel from one country to another, and they bring their music, dance and history with them. Samba is a word from Brazil. When you meet a new word like this, use the context to find its meaning. Look at the words around it: they are its clues.
explain: «...music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa.» The sentence lists samba with jazz and reggae, so samba must be a kind of music. The clue «brought from Africa» tells you that this music has roots in the traditions of people from Africa. Ask yourself: what kind of music is this? Then check that your meaning fits the sentence.
explain: Samba can also be a verb, a doing word. To samba means to dance the samba. Look at the ending to see how the verb changes: you samba today, you sambaed yesterday, and you are sambaing now. When you are not sure, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Samba is a fast, lively dance from Brazil.
fact: Samba is also the music for this dance, with strong drum rhythms.
fact: «Samba band» and «samba school» are phrases you may hear in music lessons, and children often play samba rhythms on drums and shakers.
fact: The sentence «...music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa» shows that samba has roots in African traditions.
fact: As a verb, samba means to dance the samba, and it can change to sambas, sambaed and sambaing.
exercise: What is a samba? | *A fast, lively dance from Brazil | A slow song sung in church | A kind of drum made in Italy | A game played with a ball
exercise: What does «dance the samba» mean? | *To dance the samba, a fast Brazilian dance | To sit down and play a board game | To sing a quiet lullaby | To stand still and listen to the band
exercise: In «...music such as jazz, samba and reggae grew partly from the music they brought from Africa», where did this music partly grow from? | The music of the moon | The music of a school bell | The music played in a football match | *The music brought from Africa
test: Yesterday Maria ___ with her friends at the carnival. | sambaing | samba | sambas | *sambaed
test: Which sentence uses samba as a noun meaning music? | *The band played a lively samba all night. | Maria will samba at the carnival. | We sambaed across the field yesterday. | The children samba in the playground every day.
exam: In «samba school», what does samba tell us? | *It tells us the school teaches the dance and music of Brazil | It tells us the school is a swimming pool | It tells us the children are sleeping in class | It tells us the school is in the middle of the ocean
